//? Title: MobileNet v1: Separable Filters and Measured Arithmetic
//? Subtitle: Tensor computations, explicit checks and reproducible local figures
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Depthwise filters separate spatial processing from pointwise channel mixing. We check the
linear factorization, tabulate full width/resolution budgets, inspect complete-model gradients,
then explicitly compare two CIFAR width multipliers with tensor Adam.
)
\toc

Source example: `examples/cpp/source/nn/1_convolutional_neural_networks/1.4_efficient_and_mobile_architectures/mobilenet_v1.cpp`.

> The method

A dense K×K convolution uses K²*Cin*Cout kernel products per output position. A depthwise
multiplier-one filter followed by pointwise mixing uses K²*Cin+Cin*Cout, giving

$$\frac{P_{\rm separable}}{P_{\rm dense}}=\frac{1}{C_{\rm out}}+\frac{1}{K^2}.$$
\alttext(Spatial filtering per channel plus pointwise channel mixing reduces arithmetic under factorized connectivity.)

This constraint does not represent every dense kernel. The linear fixture checks the equivalent
constrained kernel and all input/filter derivatives. Real v1 pairs put BN/ReLU6 after each filter,
so the entire nonlinear pair cannot be collapsed into one dense linear convolution. Full v1 uses
a 3×3/2 stem and thirteen depthwise/pointwise pairs. Width alpha truncates channels to int(alpha*c),
minimum one. Resolution changes actual output sides; ideal quadratic scaling has rounding effects.

All seven width/resolution presets below are checked, as is the full dense-3×3 baseline. Pointwise
filters dominate the full model: 94.86% of counted MACs and 74.59% of kernel weights. The stem
share is 1.91%, correcting Table 2's 1.19% typo. Weight shares exclude BN and classifier bias;
learned parameter totals include them. Full alpha-one has 4231976 learned/21888 moving scalars.
The complete backward probe uses 64-pixel synthetic inputs and shifts BN beta values to avoid
near-invisible scale directions at zero initialization. No blocks are removed.

The visible paired fit uses alpha one/.5, with 3217226/823722 learned scalars for ten classes.
Both get eight epochs here, kept short so the report runs in seconds; the original source uses
different, longer default epoch budgets for each width.
All model computations and fitting use the tensor library. BN learns scale/shift with epsilon .001;
training uses batch moments and eager moving updates use unbiased variance. He-uniform fan-in
initialization is an adaptation of the original release. Images stay at original 32×32 resolution;
only the first stride changes to one, retaining every later block/downsampling. Fits use 64 fitting,
64 validation and 64 official-test images, eight batch-eight epochs (64 updates) of Adam(.001), explicit kernel
L2=.0001/2 and independent padded crops/flips. Validation selects paired weights/moving moments
before test evaluation. These tiny CPU fits differ from both the larger source runs and published
ImageNet schedules. MAC counts are arithmetic budgets from measured output shapes, not latency.

The editable concept diagram is `diagrams/mobilenet_v1_concept.tikz`. Regeneration uses
LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, plus dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/mobilenet_v1_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/mobilenet_v1_concept.tikz > build/mobilenet_v1_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/mobilenet_v1_concept.svg build/mobilenet_v1_concept.dvi > build/mobilenet_v1_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered local concept diagram.\n'
```
